{"chapter": 7, "sha256": "c752e646248e820bbc26136b833920a454885c770cd4fe29c602127f2fdb0932", "voice": {"voice_id": "ppLqTilh7rH7fbUVlXsf", "name": "David - Documentary", "model_id": "eleven_v3", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.5, "similarity_boost": 0.8, "style": 0.0, "use_speaker_boost": true}}, "source": "docs/script.md", "files": {"narration": {"file": "ch7.mp3", "text": "About 12,900 years ago the warming stopped and the northern world fell back into cold for over a thousand years: the Younger Dryas. Hancock cites about a hundred peer-reviewed studies blaming a comet, with glass melted above 2,200 degrees at Abu Hureyra. Thirteen specialists called that refuted in 2023.\n\nGöbekli Tepe, in south-east Turkey, is a set of rings of T-shaped pillars built about 9500 BCE, seven thousand years before the Great Pyramid. Its builders, the excavators say, were hunters and foragers with no farms, pottery or metal. Klaus Schmidt thought they filled it in on purpose; later excavators say slope slides did.\n\nSchoch points to Göbekli Tepe when asked where the older civilisation was. If non-farmers could raise this, a plan at Giza in the eleventh millennium BCE is not absurd, and for this piece that makes the rest worth taking seriously. Nothing found there links it to Egypt.", "seconds": 71.16, "alignment": {"characters": ["A", "b", "o", "u", "t", " ", "1", "2", ",", "9", "0", "0", " ", "y", "e", "a", "r", "s", " ", "a", "g", "o", " ", "t", "h", "e", " ", "w", "a", "r", "m", "i", "n", "g", " ", "s", "t", "o", "p", "p", "e", "d", " ", "a", "n", "d", " ", "t", "h", "e", " ", "n", "o", "r", "t", "h", "e", "r", "n", " ", "w", "o", "r", "l", "d", " ", "f", "e", "l", "l", " ", "b", "a", "c", "k", " ", "i", "n", "t", "o", " ", "c", "o", "l", "d", " ", "f", "o", "r", " ", "o", "v", "e", "r", " ", "a", " ", "t", "h", "o", "u", "s", "a", "n", "d", " ", "y", "e", "a", "r", "s", ":", " ", "t", "h", "e", " ", "Y", "o", "u", "n", "g", "e", "r", " ", "D", "r", "y", "a", "s", ".", " ", "H